/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (Retrieval-Augmented Generation \(RAG\) systems have transitioned from flat, vector-based similarity lookups to structure-aware Graph-RAG frameworks capable of addressing complex, multi-hop semantic queries. The recently proposed LightRAG architecture \(2024\) significantly optimized indexing overhead and dual-level context retrieval through incremental localized graph updates. However, when deployed in enterprise-scale production environments under 2026 workloads, LightRAG reveals critical system bottlenecks: substantial memory overhead due to its in-memory graph dependencies, a high susceptibility to retrieval failure from lexical-semantic gaps during keyword extraction, and downstream context window bloat. This paper introduces Enterprise-LightRAG, a production-grade, high-throughput evolution of the original framework. We replace the volatile in-memory storage layer with a native, distributed Neo4j graph database instance, decoupling graph topology from system RAM. To resolve entry-point retrieval failures, we implement a Hybrid Lexical-Semantic Query Track that merges BM25 keyword matching with dense vector lookups via Reciprocal Rank Fusion \(RRF\). Furthermore, an asynchronous concurrency layer managed by Celery and Redis is introduced to throttle API consumption, alongside an LLMLingua token compression layer to prune context bloat before generation. We establish a deterministic evaluation framework using Ragas across ultra-large technical and legal corpora. Our structural modifications show significant systemic improvements in throughput, latency, and context utilization efficiency over the baseline model.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
